WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.850
Much the generally-accepted knowledge about
Watchmen and its making would likely be loudly

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.420
shouted down by the book's creators, Alan
Moore and Dave Gibbons.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.500
The inspirations for Watchmen, its creators'
intentions, and the contributions each brought

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.100
to the table are often misunderstood or overlooked.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:23.280
It's a strange irony that in a medium as concerned
with images as comic books, the writers tend

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.940
to get most of the attention.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.340
Watchmen is no exception.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.280
As the comic's artist Dave Gibbons said when
he spoke to Wired in 2008:

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.440
"People unacquainted with graphic novels,
including journalists, tend to think of Watchmen

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.800
as a book by Alan Moore that happens to have
some illustrations.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.840
And that does a disservice to the entire form."

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.640
While being interviewed by Neil Gaiman in
1987, Gibbons said it was his idea for the

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:54.440
cover of Watchmen #1 to be of the now iconic
blood-smeared smiley face.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.160
It was Gibbons, with Moore's input, who designed
the physical appearance of the characters.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:04.120
And perhaps most significantly, it was Gibbons
who brought life to Watchmen's world.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.160
Watchmen was one of a number of superhero
comic book series published in the mid-'80s

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.450
that are often associated with one another
because they're said to explore the notion

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.880
of superheroes as fascist figures.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.860
Examples include Batman: The Dark Knight Returns
and Marvel's Squadron Supreme, in which superheroes

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.320
with good intentions nevertheless turn the
U.S. into a totalitarian state.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:33.180
With brutal scenes in Watchmen of the Comedian
and Dr. Manhattan killing Vietnamese soldiers

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:38.380
and civilians, Rorschach torturing victims
for information, or the Minutemen using their

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:44.060
abilities and technology to break up protests,
it's easy to see why readers make the association

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:44.740
with fascism.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:51.060
But in a 1987 interview, Alan Moore said his
Watchmen characters weren't fascists.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.800
"Rorschach's not a fascist; he's a nutcase.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.640
The Comedian's not a fascist; he's a psychopath.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.880
Dr. Manhattan's not a fascist; he's a space
cadet.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.400
They're not fascists.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.380
They're not in control of their world."

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:09.540
Arguably, Moore's stated intentions were much
more damning toward superheroes than any series

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:10.780
before or since.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.780
Moore said he and Gibbons weren't trying to
reveal a fascist leaning in superheroes:

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.840
"Our intention was to show how superheroes
could deform the world just by being there,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.660
not that they’d have to take it over, just
their presence there would make the difference."

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.560
When talking about what is unique about Watchmen,
the darker subject matter and the violence

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:31.840
are the focus for most.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.520
What's too often forgotten are the visual
innovations it brought to comic books.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.120
Take the covers, which were devoid of fight
scenes or heroes posing.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:45.720
They included a Rorschach drawing, a torn
black-and-white photo, and a spinning perfume

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:46.220
bottle.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.200
Each cover was part of the first panel of
each issue.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.200
In a 1987 interview, Dave Gibbons said:

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:58.280
"The cover of the Watchmen is in the real
world and looks quite real, but it's starting

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.580
to turn into a comic book, a portal to another
dimension."

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.640
A perfect example was its fifth issue, titled
"Fearful Symmetry."

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:11.700
Each page and panel of Watchmen #5 was designed
to act as a mirror to its opposite.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.820
Likewise, the scenes of each page mirror their
opposites.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.480
It isn't a secret that the psychotic Rorschach
was based on the former Charlton Comics hero

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.680
known as the Question.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.180
But what you may not know is that Rorschach
and his predecessor had a chance to meet one

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:26.680
another.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:27.600
Kind of.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.100
In The Question #17, Vic Sage, a.k.a.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:37.080
The Question, finds himself leaning toward
more brutal tactics, lamenting the opportunities

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.360
he's given his enemies because he supposedly
hasn't dealt with them harshly enough.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.220
While in pursuit of a fugitive, Sage takes
a plane to Seattle and picks up a copy of

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:49.420
Watchmen at the terminal newsstand to read
on the flight.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.180
He doesn't like Rorschach's bigotry, but he's
impressed with his skills in violence.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:59.620
He falls asleep on the flight and dreams of
the death of a private eye he knew except

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.440
in the dream, Sage is Rorschach instead of
the Question.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:08.670
When he lands and starts working on his case,
he finds himself asking, "What would Rorschach

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:09.160
do?"

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.480
When a crook takes him out with a gun-butt
to the head, Sage utters Rorschach's signature

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:15.280
"Hurm."

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.900
Toward the end of the comic, he regrets trying
to emulate the more ultraviolent hero.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:25.360
When he's cornered by the crooks and they
ask if he has any last words, Sage responds,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:26.120
"Yeah.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.340
Rorschach sucks."

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:33.500
HBO's Watchmen debuted to rave reviews, and
pretty quickly, a couple of things became

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:34.840
clear about the show.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:40.140
First, the events of the series take place
decades after the events of the original story.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:45.780
Second, and more importantly, HBO's Watchmen
is a continuation of the comic book story

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:46.800
rather than the movie.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.700
A brief, memorable scene in the first episode
confirms it.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:56.880
As Angela Abar drives her son home from school,
the city of Tulsa is pelted with a heavy torrent

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.880
of tiny squid-like creatures.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:16.100
In the Watchmen graphic novel, we learn Adrian
Veidt, aka Ozymandias, is behind a conspiracy

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.560
to violently bring about a utopia.

00:05:18.889 --> 00:05:23.780
Veidt recruits the world's best scientists
to create a fake alien behemoth that he then

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:28.219
transports to New York City the hope being
that the world's powers will stop fighting

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.020
each other and unite to face this new threat.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.620
In the 2009 film, this was changed so that
Veidt manufactures an attack that appears

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.480
to be from Doctor Manhattan, with the same
results.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:45.900
Since we know the alien squid isn't in the
film, HBO's Watchmen can only be a continuation

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:46.960
of the graphic novel.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.680
It could be these squid storms will be what
uncovers Veidt's conspiracy, and we're already

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.340
seeing hints of that.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:58.940
In Watchmen's second episode, a news vendor
is spouting conspiracies to a man delivering

00:05:58.940 --> 00:05:59.720
newspapers.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:04.080
One of the things Watchmen is known for is
its alternate history.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.399
In the graphic novel, Richard Nixon is serving
his fifth term as president, and the civil

00:06:09.399 --> 00:06:14.249
rights battles won in the '60s and '70s never
happened because the superheroes were there

00:06:14.249 --> 00:06:15.920
to stop the protests.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.280
So when the first episode of Watchmen opens
with a 1921 race massacre on the streets of

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.720
Tulsa, you might think this is more fictional
world-building, but it isn't.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:31.270
The Tulsa race massacre happened in the real
world, and if anything, Watchmen only shows

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:32.240
the tip of the iceberg.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:38.340
The 1921 Tulsa massacre has been largely lost
from our history books because of systematic

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.240
attempts to cover up the events.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.120
The Los Angeles Times reported:

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:46.420
"In the aftermath of the killings, attempts
were made to cover up the events.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:51.120
Stories were removed from newspaper archives,
and some official accounts were destroyed."

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:56.459
This is likely part of the reason why the
official death count of the massacre is 36,

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:02.400
but more recent estimates put the number closer
to 300 killed, along with around 800 injured.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:07.369
According to the Times story, the massacre
began after news circulated that a black man

00:07:07.369 --> 00:07:11.770
had assaulted a young white woman in an elevator,
though later reports suggested he may have

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:13.960
tripped and fallen onto the woman on accident.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:20.180
The white rioters, supported by the Ku Klux
Klan, not only killed and injured black citizens,

00:07:20.189 --> 00:07:25.009
but they destroyed the black-owned businesses
of Tulsa, places so successful that Booker

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:29.480
T. Washington had coined the community as
the, quote, "Black Wall Street of America."

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:34.219
If you're familiar with the graphic novel,
the movie, or both, then one of the biggest

00:07:34.219 --> 00:07:39.180
differences you'll notice right away is that
in HBO's Watchmen, the masked men and women

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:40.680
are working with the cops.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:45.999
In the original story, the Keene Act makes
masked vigilantes illegal except for those

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.000
working for the government.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:51.500
"Congress is pushing through some new bill
that's gonna outlaw masks.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:52.600
Our days are numbered."

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:58.459
The only heroes we see employed by the feds
are the Comedian, Doctor Manhattan, and Silk

00:07:58.460 --> 00:07:59.100
Spectre.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.419
The rest of the heroes are either officially
retired or, like Rorschach, renegades who

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.320
the police would love to get their hands on.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:11.940
So it's something of a shock when, with the
exception of the Rorschach-inspired Cavalry,

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:16.040
all of the masked people of HBO's Watchmen
are police.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:21.220
It seems likely this is a result of the White
Night, an event that took place some years

00:08:21.229 --> 00:08:25.719
before the events of the series, where the
Cavalry broke into the homes of most of the

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:29.900
Tulsa PD and killed the officers and their
families as they slept.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:35.180
Most of the survivors immediately resigned,
though Angela and Chief Judd Crawford refused

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:35.840
to back down.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:41.220
So presumably part of the reason for the masks
is to protect the officers and their families.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:46.519
But certain officers apparently get special
designation and rise above the yellow-masked

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:47.320
rank and file.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:53.500
There's Angela, aka Sister Knight, Looking
Glass, the Red Scare, Pirate Jenny, and Panda.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.760
At least one character from HBO's Watchmen
also appeared in the graphic novel: Adrian

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.880
Veidt, aka Ozymandias.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:07.500
In the first episode, Veidt enjoys himself
on a vast, idyllic castle property that he

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:10.110
appears to share with two beloved servants.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:14.620
They serve him a cake with a single candle
and gift him with an old pocket watch.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:19.320
He tells them he's written a play he wants
the pair to star in called The Watchmaker's

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:19.820
Son.

00:09:20.329 --> 00:09:24.680
This is a little strange already, because
you'd think in his waning years, the man who

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:27.560
believes he saved the world would want to
write about himself.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:33.180
But if you know Watchmen, then you know the
Watchmaker's son can only be Doctor Manhattan.

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:36.060
Things get a lot weirder in the second episode.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.220
We learn who we thought were Veidt's only
servants are part of a series of identical

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:41.740
clones.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:47.220
Veidt watches a rehearsal of his play, which
climaxes with the accident that turns Jon

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:52.120
Osterman into Doctor Manhattan, including
pyrotechnics that kill the actor playing him.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.620
"Three, two, one."

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:05.970
No one seems particularly upset with this,
and Veidt directs his clones to put the dead

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.940
actor in the basement with the others.

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:13.540
What's not as dramatic but still noteworthy
is that his servants bring him another cake,

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:15.140
but this time with two candles.

00:10:15.629 --> 00:10:20.260
Assuming these events happen concurrent to
what's going on in Tulsa, we know a year hasn't

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:25.260
passed, which suggests the cake isn't a birthday
cake after all, but something else.

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:30.480
The image of the watch recurs over and over
again in the Watchmen graphic novel.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.660
Each chapter ends with a ticking clock.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:37.660
The fourth chapter, "Watchmaker," goes back
and forth in time through the lens of Doctor

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.780
Manhattan's non-linear point of view.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:44.760
The symbol on Doctor Manhattan's forehead
looks something like a watch face, though

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:50.000
it's meant to be an atom, as does the blood-splattered
smiley face that's become so iconic in reference

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.000
to Watchmen.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:55.400
HBO's Watchmen continues this tradition, in
images and sounds.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:01.400
There's, of course, the foreboding "tick tock"
chant of the Rorschach-inspired Seventh Kavalry.

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:11.480
"Tick tock, tick tock, tick tock, tick tock,
tick tock, tick tock, tick tock..."

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:17.230
When the Tulsa PD raids a Cavalry safehouse,
we learn the group is using old lithium batteries

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:19.320
from watches to build some kind of weapon.

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:25.000
And there's the pocket watch Adrian Veidt's
servants give to him as a present, along with

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:27.420
the name of his play, The Watchmaker's Son.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:28.720
What does it all mean?

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:33.300
On one hand, just as in the graphic novel,
the watches serve as a countdown to something

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:33.800
cataclysmic.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:39.780
This is also likely the reason why the candles
on Veidt's cakes increase by one each episode.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:44.280
On the other hand, Watchmen has always been
very concerned with time and how we perceive

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:48.840
it, so the watches are a constant reminder
of our strange relationship with time.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.980
HBO's Watchmen hasn't forgotten its roots,
and the evidence is everywhere.

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:58.200
There are plenty of callbacks to the graphic
novel, including technological advances clearly

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.180
inspired by the heroes of the source material.

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:05.500
In the first episode, after some of the Kavalry
members try to escape with their homemade

00:12:05.509 --> 00:12:11.560
bomb in a small plane, Judd and Pirate Jenny
follow in what appears to be the Owlship from

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:12.140
the comic.

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:17.620
In the second episode, we see someone in a
winged get-up inspired by the Watchmen hero

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:18.120
Mothman.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:22.900
He falls out of the sky while the police are
investigating the crime scene where Judd was

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:26.680
hanged, and the Red Scare beats the winged
interloper for his trouble.

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:31.980
Apparently in the world of Watchmen, Mothman
technology is their version of drones.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:36.720
There's also a "historical" show about the
Minutemen called American Hero.

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:42.650
By the second episode, we get an entire bloody,
brutal scene involving Hooded Justice and

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:43.560
a group of robbers.

00:12:44.029 --> 00:12:48.540
The hero was only seen in flashbacks in the
Watchmen graphic novel, and he's a perfect

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:49.240
addition here.

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:56.120
Considering his hood, the noose imagery, Judd's
death, and his seeming secret KKK past, there's

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:59.840
something disturbingly poignant about Hooded
Justice in this narrative.

